¶
{{smaller block|‘Twould ’''braid'' yourself too near for me to tell it.
{{right|''Shak.'', Pericles, i. 1.}}}}

'''Braided rug''', a rug or mat for the floor, formed by braiding strips of woolen or silk fabrics, and afterward sewing them together.—'''To braid St. Catherine’s tresses''', to live a virgin.
{{smaller block|Thou art too fair to ''braid St. Catharine’s tresses.''
{{right|''Longfellow'', Evangeline, ii. 1.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1†.''' To move quickly; start; rush.
{{smaller block|Whan she saugh tweyne come hir to socour, she ''braied'' rudely oute of theire handes. {{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), iii. 464.}}
Troilus . . . disposed wod out of his wit to ''breyde.''
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, iv. 230.}}}}
'''2†.''' To start suddenly (out of sleep); awake.
{{smaller block|With the falle right out of slepe she ''brayde.''
{{right|''Chaucer'', Reeve’s Tale, l. 365.}}}}
'''3.''' To nauseate; desire to vomit. [Prov. Eng.]—'''4.''' To be like; resemble in appearance or character. [Prov. Eng.]

'''braid'''<sup>1</sup> (brād), ''n.'' [< ME. ''braid'', ''breid'', < AS. ''bræd'', ''bred'' (for *''brægd'', *''bregd''), trick, deceit, ''gebregd'', quick motion, trick, deceit (= Icel. ''bragdh'', a quick motion, trick, scheme), < ''bregdan'' = Icel. ''bregdha'', move quickly, etc.: see ''braid''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] '''1†.''' A quick motion; a start.
{{smaller block|She waketh, walwith, maketh many a ''brayde.''
{{right|''Chaucer'', Good Women, l. 1164.}}}}
'''2†.''' A moment.
{{smaller block|<poem>But curtois, debonair, and vertuous;
Hyt appered well by hys workes eche ''braide.''</poem>
{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S), l. 6239.}}
For as I sodainely went in hand therewith, and made it in a ''breide.'' {{right|''Sir T. More'', Works (1557).}}}}

